# Eventual Consistency Problems & Solutions 🚀
> **Goal:** Understand the problems caused by Eventual Consistency and the common techniques used by large-scale systems (Google, Meta, Amazon, Netflix) to improve user experience.

---

# What is Eventual Consistency?

In distributed systems, data is replicated across multiple servers.

When a write happens, **all replicas are NOT updated immediately.**

Instead,

```
Write

↓

Primary Updated

↓

Background Replication

↓

Replica 1 Updated

↓

Replica 2 Updated

↓

Replica 3 Updated
```

Until every replica receives the update,

**different replicas may have different data.**

Eventually...

```
Primary == Replica1 == Replica2 == Replica3
```

Hence the name

> **Eventual Consistency**

---

# Why do we use Eventual Consistency?

Because Strong Consistency is expensive.

Strong Consistency

```
Every replica must update

↓

Then reply

↓

Slow
```

Eventual Consistency

```
Primary updates

↓

Immediately reply

↓

Replicas sync later

↓

Fast
```

Trade-off

```
Fast Performance

vs

Latest Data
```

Modern companies usually choose

✅ Better Performance

and solve consistency issues using smart techniques.

---

# Problems in Eventual Consistency

There are mainly **3 major user-facing problems**.

```
Eventual Consistency
        │
        ├── Reading Your Own Writes
        ├── Monotonic Reads
        └── Consistent Prefix Reads
```

---

# 1. Reading Your Own Writes (RYOW)

## Problem

You update your data.

Immediately after,

you read it.

But you receive old data.

---

## Real Life Example

Instagram Profile

You change your profile picture.

```
Old DP

↓

Upload New DP

↓

Success
```

Now you refresh.

Expected

```
New DP
```

Actual

```
Old DP 😐
```

Why?

Because

```
Write

↓

Primary Updated

↓

Replica NOT Updated Yet

↓

Read goes to Replica

↓

Old Data Returned
```

---

## Visualization

```
            Write

User ----------------> Primary

                          |

                    New Name = Alex

                          |

                    Replication Running

                          |

User reads -----------------------> Replica

                           Old Name = John
```

User gets confused.

"I just changed it!"

---

## Why does this happen?

Because

```
Write

↓

Primary

↓

Read

↓

Replica
```

Different databases.

Replica is still behind.

---

# Solution 1 : Read From Primary (Session Consistency)

Simple idea

After a user performs a write,

temporarily force all reads

to go to the same server

that accepted the write.

```
User

↓

Write

↓

Primary

↓

Read

↓

Primary
```

No stale data.

---

## Real Life Example

Changing Password

```
Change Password

↓

Immediately Login

↓

Always verify from Primary
```

Imagine logging in with the new password but the replica still has the old password.

That would be terrible.

---

## Pros

✅ Always sees latest data

Simple implementation

---

## Cons

Primary receives extra load.

Cannot route every read to replicas.

---

# Solution 2 : Timestamp Based Reads

Instead of always reading from Primary,

remember

**when the write happened.**

Example

```
Write Time

10:30:15
```

Now while reading,

Replica says

```
Latest Data

10:30:10
```

Oops.

Replica is behind.

System waits

or

redirects request to Primary.

---

## Visualization

```
Client

Write Timestamp = 105

↓

Replica Version = 101

↓

101 < 105

↓

Replica Behind

↓

Read from Primary
```

---

## Used In

Large distributed databases.

Useful when

```
Many Replicas

Many Regions
```

---

# Summary

```
Problem

You cannot read your own update.

↓

Reason

Read reached stale replica.

↓

Solutions

Read Primary

or

Timestamp Check
```

---

# 2. Monotonic Reads

## Definition

A user should never observe time moving backward.

Every new read should be

at least as fresh

as the previous one.

---

## Real Life Example

WhatsApp

Unread Messages

Suppose

Replica A

```
100 Messages
```

Replica B

```
95 Messages
```

User first connects to Replica A.

```
Messages = 100
```

Next request goes to Replica B.

```
Messages = 95
```

Looks like

```
100

↓

95
```

Time moved backwards.

Impossible.

---

## Another Example

Instagram Followers

```
Read 1

Followers = 5012

↓

Next Refresh

Followers = 5008
```

Nobody unfollowed.

Replica was stale.

---

## Visualization

```
Replica A

Version 20

↓

Replica B

Version 18

↓

User

20

↓

18 ❌
```

---

# Solution

Always route one user

to the same replica.

Example

```
Replica = UserID % NumberOfReplicas
```

```
User 21

↓

21 % 3

↓

Replica 0
```

Every request goes there.

---

## Why does this work?

Because

Same replica

↓

Same data progression

↓

No backward movement

---

## Real Life Analogy

Imagine watching a movie.

First half

```
Netflix Server A
```

Second half suddenly switches

to

```
Server B

20 minutes behind
```

Movie starts going backward.

Obviously bad experience.

---

## Advantages

Simple

Predictable

No backward reads

Better user experience

---

## Disadvantages

One replica may become overloaded.

If that replica fails,

user must switch to another.

---

# Summary

```
Problem

Reads go backward.

↓

Reason

Different replicas.

↓

Solution

Sticky Replica

(UserID Hash)

Same replica every time.
```

---

# 3. Consistent Prefix Reads

## Definition

If Event A happened before Event B,

users should never see

B before A.

Order must remain correct.

---

## Real Life Example

WhatsApp Chat

Person A

```
Lunch chale?
```

Person B replies

```
Haan
```

Correct Order

```
Lunch chale?

↓

Haan
```

Imagine seeing

```
Haan

↓

Lunch chale?
```

Makes no sense.

---

## Why does this happen?

Suppose

Messages stored in different partitions.

```
Partition 1

Lunch chale?

↓

Delayed
```

```
Partition 2

Haan

↓

Fast
```

Replica receives

```
Haan

↓

Lunch chale?
```

Wrong order.

---

## Visualization

```
Message A

↓

Partition 1

↓

Delayed

--------------------

Message B

↓

Partition 2

↓

Fast
```

Receiver sees

```
B

↓

A
```

Wrong.

---

# Solution

Store causally related messages

inside the same partition.

```
Chat 101

↓

Partition 5

↓

Message A

↓

Message B
```

Since one partition processes sequentially,

order remains correct.

---

## Modern Example

Threaded Replies

```
Tweet

↓

Reply

↓

Reply to Reply
```

Usually stored together

to preserve ordering.

---

## Why Same Partition?

Within one partition

```
FIFO

(First In First Out)
```

Messages are processed

in the exact arrival order.

---

## Advantages

Correct ordering

No confusing conversations

Easy causal reasoning

---

## Disadvantages

One partition can become hot.

Large conversations

may overload one partition.

---

# Summary

```
Problem

Dependent events appear
out of order.

↓

Reason

Different partitions.

↓

Solution

Store related events
inside same partition.
```

---

